sey so as we sit here  today we've got 3 000 young men across  our schools and have graduated about 2  000 young men over the years and sent  them on to colleges and universities  across the country  we started in collaboration with 100  black men uh in new york uh really as a  response to the national crisis that we  have seen so many of our young men have  faced where they've not graduated from  high school or not presenting themselves  for opportunities in industry i'm  getting caught up in the the uh you know  getting incarcerated caught up in the  criminal justice system all the things  that we hear about on a regular basis  um but there's really been relatively uh  little response to that crisis and so  the eagle academy was created really in  many ways as a response to giving young  men a place where they can hope and  dream and and reach their their full  potential  and there's at least two really  interesting things that might be  surprising for some folks that you just  mentioned  this isn't your traditional  charter school these are actual public  schools in the new york city public  school system i guess in newark in one  case as well and then the other  surprising thing is you don't hear a lot  about you know single sex schools  anymore this is a school for uh young  men at risk young men  how first of all how does the first  thing work what if how does a branded  system of schools within the public  school system work and why did you make  that decision and then why the decision  to focus only on young men  well many years ago when uh when mike  bloomberg was the mayor of new york city  uh he cast a wide net in terms of trying  to get other not-for-profits and  organizations to get more involved in  the public education system and in doing  so um we became part of the 21st century  uh uh education reform effort that was  happening in new york sponsored by the  gates foundation um where they said  listen this is a huge system and we need  uh we need others to get involved and so  that's when our organization 100 black  men decided to get involved over the  years it had become politically  incorrect to have single gender schools  um but but we leaned into that space  really to say you know what is it that  causes young men  to believe in themselves and and to  focus on their own potential and i was  the principal of the school we created  it in the bronx with a group of ninth  grade boys and quickly realized that if  we were gonna do this well we needed to  start earlier than the ninth grade and  our model soon after became a grade six  through twelve model  but we we were intentional in saying  that we wanted it to be traditional  public schools because philosophically  we really felt as though  you can't charter your way out of this  educational arrest charters ought to be  a big part of the response in education  but over 90 percent of high school kids  in america still want a regular  traditional schools like most of us went  to and if you can't move the needle in  that space you're just playing around on  the margins and so we wanted to be able  to lean into that space and that's what  we've done  and what what does it look like what's  different about an eagle academy public  school other than the single gender  focus  what are y'all doing that you think is  different and what have you learned that  has been particularly effective  several things um it's it's about the  culture that you established that says  to young men that we deeply believe in  you and and that may seem like something  really simple um but but it is not you  have to remember that  eighty percent of teachers across  america are white and female  um and don't necessarily have  a background in it or training  and how to work with boys first of all  there's some significant differences  between the energy that boys can bring  to the classroom and that girls we can  bring to the classroom broadly  but when you start talking about um boys  of color  there are some unique challenges um  that those young men bring to the  classroom every day and there's a unique  energy that they bring and very often uh  so the boys find themselves getting  disciplined in school  are being called out for things um that  are really not bad behavior  it's it's it's just having teachers  having a level of sensitivity and  understanding  who the boys are what their backgrounds  are and and and recognizing that there's  a certain way as adults that we have to  show up if we want to get the most out  of these young men so we engage them in  all kinds of professional development  and training for our teachers around  what does it mean uh to meet the boys  where they are we've got  expanded day programming um that really  is about what are the things that boys  are really interested in right um we've  got game rooms you think about it the  boys love to play video games um  but yet in schools uh we we we don't  often  utilize the kinds of things that boys  are interested in we use a one size fits  all kind of approach boys love to  compete  but they don't like to compete  one-on-one they want to compete my group  against your group so how do you how do  you frame a spirit of competition  uh in into how you do your day-to-day  interactions in school but one of the  most important things that we do is it's  just about the culture of  saying to young men we see you we  recognize all of the challenges that you  face on a daily basis we have a daily  town hall meeting for half an hour  before the young men set foot into a  classroom  we we we we pour libation and they get a  chance just to reflect on what's  happening in their lives and the trauma  that so many of them are feeling each  and every day um that's not the kind of  thing that you can measure in a  standardized test but it's the kind of  thing that says to the young men um we  see you  and we're here for you and it makes all  the difference in the world  i can imagine that's very powerful and i  want to encourage everyone watching to  put questions on facebook and youtube  about really anything regarding  education it could be a single gender  education it could be you know this  question around  public schools charter schools it could  be how we're going to do distance  learning and we'll talk a little bit  more about that uh but david as you've  gone on this journey i guess how many  first of all how many years has it been  and what has been kind of your maybe  some of your things you've been  struggling the most with that you're  still trying to figure out and then what  have been some of the the big wins that  you're like wow that's a powerful data  point that shows that we're on the right  track  well there are a few things uh sal i  would tell you that first of all i've  been in education for over 30 years  myself having been a teacher and  assistant principal founding principal  not only the eagle academy but of a  wonderful co-ed school in the bronx as  well so this is a space that i've  dedicated my life to um there are a  number of things that we have learned in  the space for boys but we've also seen  very broadly really for boys and and for  girls um  internationally there have been some  studies that have been done around boys  and and the biggest thing that emerges  is that boys disconnect from school  because school is boring  the connection to what they're doing in  class to what their real interests are  very often are not aligned  and most teachers will tell you that  they recognize that but they struggle  with how to figure out how to how to  make that connection  um so intuitively they know that boys  check out easily um but you've got to  figure out how do you make your lessons  more fast-paced more engaging um in  order for boys to really stay focused  and they've got to be relevant you've  got to be talking about issues that  really do make a difference and that's  where i think we begin to see the  overlap between