e will just be doing the  routine of going to school every day but  not necessarily preparing young people  what they need to be successful in their  future  and so as you engage and arguably a lot  of what you're saying applies to both  genders but as you as you engage in this  way you know what what are you seeing of  your graduates are they  are they being more successful in  college are they just more confident in  their their lives  what data points have you have you seen  so far  what we have seen is something that  sometimes is difficult to measure right  and so um  uh  how do you measure grit how do you  measure determination right that's  something that people have really been  been struggling with  um  every day at  eagle academy our young men recite the  poem invictus  and a victor says the is the poem of  resilience it was the poem recited by  nelson mandela every day when he was  locked up on robin's island for 27 years  it was the favorite poem of john lewis  who just recently passed away it's about  it's a poem that really talks about when  you get knocked down how to get back up  but we talk about that poem and we  recite that poem across with all 3 000  of our young men each and every day  and and that poem really represents  what we want from our young men to know  that life is going to hit you and you're  going to get knocked down but our young  men who've gone who graduated and gone  off to colleges and universities across  the country have been able to persevere  we've been able to get our numbers  graduation numbers higher than the  national average and we believe that's  because our young men may not come out  with the highest test scores but they  come out with something inside of them  which talks about how to stay focused  that when the going gets tough you got  to continue to fight and to press on and  that your destiny um is is is is  wonderful uh and full of potential and  that is that's what the whole eagle  experience has really represented for  them and that's what we continue to push  for with we're continuing listen we're  still very much a work in progress we're  still learning every day we're very much  a learning organization um but our young  men have been doing our young men have  been doing well and doing us proud  yeah no i could imagine and a lot of  great questions coming in from social  media from facebook  kids show ricardi is asking are there  strategies already in place to educate  the parents simultaneously to engage  them to nurture their kids and the  importance of their role in their kids  life while educating nurturing the kids  i believe that parents and teachers  equally play an important role in how we  shape their thoughts actions and  upbringing  thoughts  huge part of the work that we do at  eagle one of the reasons why we've been  as successful as we have been is because  we don't just talk about parents being  involved and engaged we practice that we  let our parents know we're we are  partners in this so let me give you one  example of something that we do that  most schools across america don't do  we gather our parents  for community gatherings with their  teachers and principals on saturday  mornings  now why is that important because the  traditional wednesday thursday night  after work meeting is something that  people have been doing all across  america we we lament the fact that lots  of parents don't come out and don't get  involved well they've got other kids  they've got two or three jobs it's  really tough and that's because we  generally set our schedule of how we  want parents to be involved we set that  in a space that benefits school p  personnel  it's good for us to show up on a  wednesday night we're already in school  we just stick around for a couple of  extra hours but we had an idea that if  we met parents on saturday mornings we  could get them much more involved and it  has been absolutely amazing the response  that we've gotten from our parents when  they show up on saturdays when we've  we've encouraged them to take the lead  and so it's not just the school  principal who has to run those meetings  but the parents themselves are leading  the work and the efforts and how you  engage the community it's a huge part of  the dna of what we do and uh and a real  big part of reason why we've been so  successful with our young men because  their parents have been running this  race side by side with us  now i love that idea but a lot of the  things we've already mentioned feel like  things that  many schools can replicate uh these  aren't like you know completely  re-engineering everything making sure  that kids feel supported believe in  themselves i love invictus i so i love  the fact that y'all are uh reciting that  or invoking that every day you know  having saturday uh meeting so it's much  more it's much easier for parents to  engage a lot of questions coming in  sure from youtube smart smartbear  asks uh david  what i guess you already talked a little  bit about this why you started eagle  academy but you know what are your  what are your inspirations i mean maybe  i'll broaden the question you know  describe this path you've talked about  spending three decades in education why  did you pick you know what were you like  in in high school and middle school  what brought you down this path and you  know at the end of your career what do  you hope you can look back on and say  happened  i i didn't go to school initially to  become an educator uh i started out as  an engineering major at rutgers  university and after a couple years i  switched to political science with the  intention of going to law school which i  ultimately did i started teaching and  having a family and uh and then went to  law school at night uh left education  never thinking that i was coming back  and and worked uh for the new york city  and new york state department of law  um but as fate would have it i was  really supposed to be working with young  people and i came back and became a  school administrator uh and and i've had  a wonderful career and am absolutely  living my purpose  so let me ask you one question on that  so you were you were a practicing lawyer  and then you somehow went to become a i  mean what was i mean you know i i  famously i was a tech guy hedge fund  analyst started tutoring cousins khan  academy how did you make that transition  from being a practicing lawyer to being  a school administrator  well uh it was because i i really as a  person of faith right i just i felt my  calling  was um well as a child i thought i  always wanted to be a lawyer  but but as as i got older i realized  that my real calling was working with  young people and so i decided to go to  go back into education and um and i went  back and i got my certification to be a  school administrator in in one semester  by going to three colleges at the same  time  um  which people have  found to be somewhat inspiring um but  for me was just an example that when  you're very focused on what it is that  you're supposed to be doing in life um  you know they say the universe will  conspire for your success  and uh and doors will open if you're  prepared to put in the work and i was  prepared to put in the work so for me on  a personal level uh that's kind of how i  got to be where i where i'm at kind of  being back in this education space but i  was inspired around this work of eagle  academy because columbia university put  out a report  that said that  uh 75  of the state's prison population  was coming from seven neighborhoods in  new york city so you think about that  sound right  um 75 if you look at the entire state of  new york 75 of the inmates were coming  not just from new york city but from  seven very specific zip codes in new  york city this is where people of color  are right central harlem the low east  side southeast queens um  you know uh uh bedford stuyvesant it's  the communities where where